{"text": "1. Field of the Invention\nThis invention relates to the field of molecular biology, and in particular, to DNA sequences isolated from a Drosophila species.\n2. Description of the Related Art\nDrosophila has been and remains the species of choice for molecular biological research, because it is a powerful model organism for studying developmental biology, genetics, molecular genetics, evolution, and behavior.\nOne important and highly-developed aspect of Drosophila research concerns the development of DNA sequencing technology. This technology has been demonstrated in numerous other species, particularly those that belong to the group of insects called dipterans. For example, the complete sequencing of a honey bee was completed using this technology. In the Drosophila melanogaster species, the nucleotide sequence of the polytene chromosomes has been determined. The entire sequence of the Drosophila genome was recently completed (Koschl et al., 1995).\nThis ability to determine the nucleotide sequence of the genomes of all insects has several important applications. In the Drosophila system, sequence data can be used to deduce the evolutionary relationships between the different species (Saito et al., 1994). Furthermore, the use of nucleic acid probes can be used in gene cloning experiments. The DNA probes that were produced are designed so that they can be used to clone any specific genes of the organism into which the probe is introduced (Roulin et al., 1995). In addition, the sequence data can also be used to look for mutations in the genome of the organism (Koschl et al., 1995).\nThis invention relates to Drosophila species, which is the first Drosophila-based DNA sequence.\nThe following publications are referenced in this application by numbers within brackets: [1-4], [12-16], [17-19], [26,27], [35], [36], [37-43], and [44-46].", "depth": 1, "seed_id": 3, "token_count": 512}
{"text": "Q:\n\nWhat does $x^{1/2}-x$ mean?\n\nI was reviewing for a test this morning and I was surprised to see the answer was $x^{1/2}-x$ because it looks like the product of both roots is just the square root of a negative number. If $x^{1/2}-x$ has a negative, how do I know it isn't the product of two negative numbers. In the textbook I am reading, they use $x^{1/2}-x$ as an example of how to use the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra to solve a quadratic equation. I'm confused why they are using it to solve a quadratic and a cubic equation. I believe if $x^{1/2}-x=0$, then there is no solution to $x^{1/2}-x$, but I don't get how $x^{1/2}-x$ can be used to solve a quadratic equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$. \n\nA:\n\nOne could also use a similar approach to the textbook to obtain the answer.\nLet $f(x) = x^{1/2} - x$. Then $f$ has a simple zero at $x=1$, so by the mean value theorem, there exists a $k$ such that $f(k) = 0$. By solving $k^{1/2} - k = 0$, one gets the quadratic equation\n$$k(k^{1/2} - k) = 0 \\iff k(1-k)(1-k) = 0 \\iff k^2 = 1. $$\nThis can be simplified to\n$$k(k^{1/2} - k) = 0 \\iff k = \\frac{1}{2} \\frac{1-k}{1+k}, $$\nwhich can be simplified further to \n$$k^2 - 1 = 0 \\iff k^2 - 1 = 0 \\iff (2k-1) \\text{ is an odd integer.}$$\nIn other words, the quadratic you are given is\n$$ (2k - 1)x^2 + (2k-1)x + 1 $$\nwhere $k$ is the real root you are looking for.\n\n", "depth": 1, "seed_id": 4, "token_count": 512}
