{"text": "Q:\n\nWhat does the \"no-one\" command do?\n\nI recently came across a command which is usually called \"no-one\", but I have been unable to find the meaning of it in the context of the shell. Here is the example, from the Unix & Linux Shell book:\n$ no-one | egrep '^1$'\nno-one: command not found\n\nWhat does the command no-one do?\n\nA:\n\nIt's not a shell command. You're probably looking for:\n$ no-one | egrep '^1$'\n\nThat's the same as:\n$ no-one && egrep '^1$'\n\nThat would be the same thing as:\n$ no-one\n$ egrep '^1$'\n\nand the same as:\n$ no-one | egrep '^1$'\n\nI don't know what it does, since it's not written in the Unix/Linux Shell book. It could be something in the Bash shell, or something else.\n\n", "depth": 2, "seed_id": 4, "token_count": 512}
